explanationMd:
An experiment can only attribute a difference in its results to the things it deliberately changed if everything else is held fixed. Here the student deliberately changed exactly two things: the **water temperature** and the **identity of the salt**. Everything named in the stem is one of the things held constant:

- *distilled water* — tap water carries dissolved minerals that vary from day to day and could change how much salt dissolves;
- *the same stirring rate* — stirring affects how quickly a portion dissolves, so a faster stir in one trial could make the student stop adding salt too late;
- *the same 5-minute rule* — this fixes what counts as "saturated", so the endpoint means the same thing in every trial.

With those three fixed, a difference between two numbers in Table 4 can be traced back to temperature or to the salt, which is exactly what the study set out to compare.

Check the answer by imagining the control removed. Suppose the student had stirred the $70\;^{\circ}\text{C}$ trials harder than the $10\;^{\circ}\text{C}$ trials. Salt P's rise from $21.0$ g to $138.0$ g would then have two possible causes — the heat and the stirring — and the table could not tell them apart. That is precisely the confusion the controls prevent.
